# 00-inbox

**What this is for.** The worst outcome is a source you did not capture. When you are
moving fast, drop the file here with a correct header and move on.

The pipeline processes the inbox like any other folder, because the header decides
where a source goes, not the folder. If the filename carries a module prefix such as
`MCR_2026-03-14_...`, the tracker sync routes it automatically.

## Use this instead of guessing

Most misfiles so far came from filing into a module folder under time pressure: SEO
into measurement, advertorials into ads, brand psychology into organic, customer
experience into store setup. If you are not sure which module a source belongs to,
**drop it here**. A source in the inbox with a good header is better than a source in
the wrong folder, because the wrong folder looks finished and gets trusted.

## The only rule

Write the header before you drop it. At minimum:

```
---
url:                   # REQUIRED. Never blank. offline for books and PDFs.
rights:                # owned | public_reference | licensed | study_only
why:                   # what a good model would not say unprompted
---
```

Those 3 fields cannot be recovered later. Everything else can.

## When to use a module folder instead

When you already know where it goes and it costs you nothing to put it there. Check the
`_README` in that folder if the boundary is not obvious. The most common traps:

```
advertorials, quiz funnels             -> 07-LPG, not ads
copywriting craft                      -> 11-ADC, not creative
anything about ranking or citation     -> 15-SEO or 16-AEO, not measurement
brand meaning, positioning, naming     -> 04-BRD, not organic or ops
customer experience                    -> 17-CXP, not store setup
founder stories and brand teardowns    -> 99-multi
```

Current file counts, duplicates and holds: see `_INGESTION-STATE.yaml`.
